 [:br {}] "Start Altitude: 2548 m" [:br {}] "Vertical Drop: 600 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The 2001 super-G World Cup winner was France’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/80805"} "Régine Cavagnoud"] ", but in October 2001, while training, she collided with German ski coach Markus Anwander, and sustained a serious closed head injury, from which she succumbed two days later. With Cavagnoud out, the event was wide open, though it was contested under a pall. The early leader was Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/99471"} "Karen Putzer"] ", who posted 1:13.86. But the ninth starter was another Italian, unheralded " [:a {:href "/athletes/101285"} "Daniela Ceccarelli"] ", and she bested her teammate with 1:13.59. After the 15 seeds finished, the next starter was Croatian " [:a {:href "/athletes/99432"} "Janica Kostelić"] ", who had already won the combined in Salt Lake. Kostelić passed Putzer to move into second place, but the gold medal went to Ceccarelli in a major upset. She had only once before been on the podium in a World Cup race."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "9"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/101285"} "Daniela Ceccarelli"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]] [:td {} "1:13.59"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "16"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/99432"} "Janica Kostelić"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CRO"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CRO.png"}] "CRO"]] [:td {} "1:13.64"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [